<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aratus of Sicyon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">271–213 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Aratos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aratus of Sicyon was a Greek statesman who freed his city from its tyrant in 251 BCE, brought it into the Achaean League and led the league for over thirty years as its general. He seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acrocorinth">Acrocorinth</a> from its Macedonian garrison in 243 BCE and expanded the league across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, but when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> defeated him he called in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus III Doson</a>, reversing his anti-Macedonian policy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon.jpg" alt="Aratus of Sicyon" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by Guillaume Rouille (1518?-1589) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aratus_of_Sicyon.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
